    "section": "colors"
   },
   {
    "name": "The Hue, Glyph, Word Rule",
    "body": "No status appears by colour alone. Each one pairs its hue with a stroke glyph (check, half, pause, return, slash, cross, dash, tick) and a word.",
    "section": "colors"
   },
   {
    "name": "The Six Sizes Rule",
    "body": "Use only 12, 14, 16, 18, 28 and 36px, plus 24px for phone titles and the phone verdict. Two sizes 1px apart look like a mistake, not a hierarchy. Weight (400/500/600) and ink (text/muted/faint) carry the steps between them.",
    "section": "typography"
   },
   {
    "name": "The Tone Step Rule",
    "body": "Separate surfaces by one tone step, never by an outline, a shadow, a blur or a gradient. Hairlines exist only between rows inside a card.",
    "section": "elevation"
   }
  ],
  "dos": [
   "Do separate every surface by one tone step: frame #EDEDEC, then sheet #FFFFFF, then card #F5F5F4.",
   "Do make every control a pill (999px). Use one black primary per screen.",
   "Do set all text in Switzer on the six-step scale (12/14/16/18/28/36, with 24 on a phone).",
   "Do pair every status with its hue, its glyph and its word.",
   "Do keep blue for \"verified against fetched text\" and for focus.",
   "Do keep motion to feedback (150ms on cubic-bezier(.16, 1, .3, 1)), apart from the gate's check sequence."
